  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1214 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nMonday is still on track to be the hottest day of the upcoming week \nas the core of the ~596dam H5 ridge centers directly overhead. Highs \nnear or just above 105 degrees are likely on the Caprock, with \ntemperatures currently forecast to reach as high as 113 degrees over \nthe SE TX Panhandle and adjacent Rolling Plains. Models have trended \na bit faster with the southward retreat of the upper high Monday \nevening, with some shortwave energy possibly bringing a few late \nevening storms to the southern TX Panhandle, although a dry forecast \nis favored at the moment. Temperatures will remain well above normal \nTuesday through late week, but will be a bit cooler compared to the \nintense heat of early week. Deterministic and ensemble guidance both \nfavor a suppression of the upper ridge farther to our southwest \nmidweek and beyond, with the resulting northwest flow aloft \nresulting in a more favorable setup for daily evening storm chances \nduring the Tue-Fri timeframe. However, it is still too early to say \nwith much confidence whether any beneficial rainfall will occur with \nthis pattern shift.",
